{
  "dom_id": "step-q208830",
  "name": "Question 3",
  "problem": "The point of intersection of the lines $y=3x-1$ and $y=-4x+6$ has coordinates\n\n$\\,($\n{{field-1}}\n$,$ \n{{field-2}}\n$).$",
  "worked_solution": "The point of intersection consists of the coordinates $(x,y)$ that satisfy both equations $y=3x-1$ and $y=-4x+6.$ So, we need to solve the system of equations consisting of the two lines:\n\n$$\n\\{\\begin{aligned}y=3x-1 \\\\ y=-4x+6\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe can solve this system by substitution. Substituting the first equation $y=3x-1$ into the second equation $y=-4x+6$ yields an equation that we can solve for $x:$ \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}3x-1 & =-4x+6 \\\\ 3x & =-4x+7 \\\\ 7x & =7 \\\\ x & =1\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThis is the $x$-coordinate of the point of intersection. To find the $y$-coordinate, we substitute $x=1$ back into one of the equations, say $y=3x-1,$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}y & =3x-1 \\\\  & =3(1)-1 \\\\  & =3-1 \\\\  & =2\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the solution to the system is $x=1,\\,y=2.$ So, the coordinates of the intersection point are $(\\,1,2\\,).$",
